{"ts": 1789779466.156321, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've now swept all 38 possible third features for the 4+17 core. Feature 19 is the decisive winner at 1.270 \u2014 the runner-up is 26 at 1.182, a wide gap.\n\nLet me reason about what to predict OOS rather than searching further.\n\n**Why I'm stopping here.** The winning spec wasn't discovered by mining; it's exactly the union of the three top-ranked standalone features (4: 0.846, 17: 0.719, 19: 0.652). Each leg stands on its own with a strong univariate Sharpe, and the combination beats every leg \u2014 meaning they're carrying partly independent signal rather than one feature doing the work. That's the profile that tends to survive out-of-sample, unlike a spec assembled from individually-worthless features that happen to interact.\n\n**Shrinkage.** Still, the 1.270 is an in-sample maximum over roughly 70 evaluations, with sign choices also fit in-sample, so it is biased upward. The bar's 1.017 is itself the honest estimate of what pure search noise buys in this space. Two corrections apply:\n\n- *Selection bias.* At n=5000 daily obs (~20y), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly $\\sqrt{252/5000} \\approx 0.22$. Searching ~70 specs and taking the max inflates the estimate by on the order of 0.2\u20130.3.\n- *Correlated search.* My candidates were heavily overlapping (all sharing the 4+17 core), so the effective number of independent trials is well below 70 \u2014 the inflation is at the lower end of that range. The fact that 19 beat 25 alternatives by a margin (1.270 vs 1.182) larger than typical noise also suggests a real edge, not just a lucky draw.\n\nNetting these out, I'll center my belief near 1.0 \u2014 a meaningful haircut from 1.270, landing just under the bar. My uncertainty must cover the possibility that the feature relationships shift in the holdout period (regime change would hit all three legs at once, since they're combined with fixed equal weights and no re-fitting), so I'll use a wide sd of 0.33 rather than the ~0.22 pure sampling error."}
