{"ts": 1789779512.835079, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Result\n\n**Submitted:** features 4, 17, 19 \u2014 all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.270** (bar: 1.017). **Predicted OOS: 1.0 \u00b1 0.33.**\n\n### How I got there\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Three stood clearly above the pack: 4 (0.846), 17 (0.719), 19 (0.652). Next tier was ~0.49.\n2. **Tested pairs** among the leaders and the best short-side candidates (26, 31). Best pair: 4+17 at 1.095.\n3. **Exhaustively swept all 38 remaining features** as the third leg on the 4+17 core, each with its favorable sign. Feature 19 won at 1.270, ahead of runner-up 26 at 1.182.\n4. **Verified all four sign configurations** \u2014 all-positive dominated decisively (next best flip: 0.538), confirming the signs aren't a knife-edge fit.\n\n### The main judgment call\nThe headline number is an in-sample maximum over ~70 evaluations, so it's biased upward and I deliberately did **not** submit 1.270 as my prediction.\n\nWhat makes me willing to trust this spec is *how* it was found: it's simply the three best standalone features combined, not a lucky interaction dug out of individually-useless inputs. Each leg earns its place independently, and the whole exceeds every part \u2014 evidence of genuinely complementary signal. Specs of that shape travel; ones mined from noise don't.\n\nAgainst that, I applied a haircut of ~0.27 for selection bias, tempered by the fact that my search was highly correlated (nearly every candidate shared the 4+17 core), so the effective number of independent trials \u2014 and thus the inflation \u2014 is smaller than the raw count suggests. I widened the sd to 0.33, above the ~0.22 pure sampling error at n=5000, because the three legs are combined with fixed equal weights and no refitting: a regime shift would degrade all of them together rather than independently.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: my central estimate of 1.0 sits just *below* the 1.017 bar. I take the bar seriously as a calibration of what pure search noise is worth in this space, and I'd rather report an honest number than one flattering enough to clear it.\n\n**Note on tooling:** partway through, the environment reported that a large set of claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Drive, Linear, etc.) require OAuth authorization, which can't be completed in a non-interactive session. None were needed here \u2014 the task used only `evaluate`, `status`, and `submit`. If you want those connectors available in future runs, they'd need authorizing via your claude.ai connector settings, or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
